Subject: [PATCH] g_iphone: honour the class requests our own descriptors promise
Upstream-Status: Pending

f_iphone_setup() handles standard HID descriptor reads and five vendor
requests, and stalls everything else - including every class-specific
request. One of those we are not entitled to stall, and the rest deserve
to at least be visible in a log.

UAC1 SET_CUR(SAMPLING_FREQ_CONTROL) arrives at the isochronous IN endpoint
as bmRequestType=0x22 bRequest=0x01 wValue=0x0100 wIndex=0x0081 wLength=3.
interfaces.h describes that endpoint with a UAC_EP_GENERAL class descriptor
whose bmAttributes has bit D0 set:

    } __packed cs_ep1 = {
        .header = { .bLength = 7, .bDescriptorType = USB_DT_CS_ENDPOINT },
        .raw = { 0x01, 0x01, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00 },
                  ^^^^  ^^^^ bmAttributes: sampling frequency control
                  UAC_EP_GENERAL

and interfaces_audio.h offers nine discrete sample rates in cs_intf5. Per
UAC1 5.2.3.2.3.1 a host may then select one, and stalling that request
contradicts the descriptors we just handed out. The vendor firmware accepts
it in f_audio_setup(). SET_IDLE is added for the same reason: no data
stage, every HID device is expected to accept it, and the vendor firmware's
f_hid does.

This is a correctness fix and nothing more. It is explicitly NOT a fix for
catplay-firmware issue #1: an MBUX capture (catplay issue #11) pauses only
464ms after the very same stall, while a NAC Wave 2 capture pauses 11.5s,
so the stall cannot be what dominates either case.

What both captures do have in common is a failed iAP2-over-HID handshake
just before the head unit stops making progress - the NAC walking report
IDs 1..12 with GET_REPORT, the MBUX pushing report 0x0f with SET_REPORT,
all of it stalled. That is catplay issue #11, it needs a second iAP2 pipe
running before the role switch, and it is deliberately out of scope here.
Those requests keep being stalled; answering them with an empty report the
way Android's f_hid does would hand the head unit a malformed iAP2 packet
instead of an honest "not supported", which may well be worse.

What changes for them is only that every refused request now says so, with
its bmRequestType and bRequest, so a capture shows exactly what we say no
to instead of leaving a silent gap in the log.

Note on the calling convention: composite_setup() in this tree jumps
straight to `done` after f->setup() and skips `check_value`, so a function
queues its own ep0 request and returns the usb_ep_queue() result; a
negative return stalls. ep0_recv() follows ep0_send()/ep0_zlp() in that.

NOT YET VERIFIED: this has not been compiled or run, and it was written
against catplay-labs/catplay master. Check it applies to the bundle in
.sources/catplay before trusting the diff.

---
 f_iphone.c |  94 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 94 insertions(+)

--- a/f_iphone.c
+++ b/f_iphone.c
@@ -6,6 +6,14 @@
 #define IPHONE_REQ_USBOOT 0x88
 #define IPHONE_REQ_GADGET 0x99
 
+/* USB Audio Class 1.0, section 5.2.3 */
+#define IPHONE_UAC_SET_CUR 0x01
+#define IPHONE_UAC_EP_SAMPLING_FREQ_CONTROL 0x01
+
+/* HID 1.11, section 7.2 */
+#define IPHONE_HID_GET_REPORT 0x01
+#define IPHONE_HID_SET_IDLE 0x0a
+
 static struct g_iphone *g_iphone_from_func(struct usb_function *f)
 {
 	struct f_iphone *iphone_func = container_of(f, struct f_iphone, func);
@@ -141,6 +149,23 @@
 	return usb_ep_queue(cdev->gadget->ep0, cdev->req, GFP_ATOMIC);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Accept a host-to-device data stage on ep0 and discard it. The direction of
+ * the transfer is taken from the SETUP packet by the UDC, so queueing a
+ * request of wLength bytes receives them and completes the status stage.
+ */
+static int ep0_recv(struct usb_composite_dev *cdev, u16 wLength)
+{
+	if (!cdev || !cdev->req || !cdev->gadget || !cdev->gadget->ep0)
+		return -ENODEV;
+
+	cdev->req->length = wLength;
+	cdev->req->zero = 0;
+	pr_info("iPhone: ep0_recv <- len %u", wLength);
+
+	return usb_ep_queue(cdev->gadget->ep0, cdev->req, GFP_ATOMIC);
+}
+
 /* ----------------- Control requests ----------------- */
 static int f_iphone_setup(struct usb_function *f,
 						const struct usb_ctrlrequest *ctrl)
@@ -243,6 +268,75 @@
 		}
 		}
 	}
+
+	/* Class-specific requests */
+	if ((ctrl->bRequestType & USB_TYPE_MASK) == USB_TYPE_CLASS)
+	{
+		/*
+		 * UAC1 SET_CUR(SAMPLING_FREQ_CONTROL) on the isochronous IN
+		 * endpoint. interfaces.h advertises a UAC_EP_GENERAL descriptor
+		 * with bmAttributes bit D0 set and interfaces_audio.h offers
+		 * nine discrete sample rates, so a head unit is entitled to
+		 * select one - and per UAC1 5.2.3.2.3.1 it must be able to.
+		 *
+		 * We have nothing to reconfigure, the stream is produced to
+		 * match, but the request has to be accepted rather than
+		 * stalled: stalling it contradicts our own descriptors.
+		 *
+		 * This is a correctness fix, not a known cure for anything: an
+		 * MBUX capture (catplay issue #11) shows the head unit pausing
+		 * only 464ms after the same stall, while a NAC Wave 2 capture
+		 * (catplay-firmware issue #1) pauses 11.5s. Same stall, very
+		 * different consequence - so something else dominates.
+		 */
+		if (ctrl->bRequestType == (USB_DIR_OUT | USB_TYPE_CLASS | USB_RECIP_ENDPOINT) &&
+			ctrl->bRequest == IPHONE_UAC_SET_CUR &&
+			(wValue >> 8) == IPHONE_UAC_EP_SAMPLING_FREQ_CONTROL)
+		{
+			pr_info("iPhone: audio SET_CUR sampling freq on ep 0x%02x", wIndex & 0xff);
+			return ep0_recv(cdev, wLength);
+		}
+
+		/*
+		 * HID SET_IDLE carries no data and every HID device is expected
+		 * to accept it. Acknowledge it instead of stalling.
+		 */
+		if (ctrl->bRequestType == (USB_DIR_OUT | USB_TYPE_CLASS | USB_RECIP_INTERFACE) &&
+			ctrl->bRequest == IPHONE_HID_SET_IDLE)
+		{
+			pr_info("iPhone: HID SET_IDLE (intf %u)", wIndex);
+			return ep0_zlp(cdev);
+		}
+
+		/*
+		 * Everything else stays stalled, but say so explicitly - and be
+		 * clear about what is being refused here, because it matters.
+		 *
+		 * HID GET_REPORT (0xa1/0x01) and HID SET_REPORT (0x21/0x09) are
+		 * the transport for iAP2-over-HID, which head units use before
+		 * the role switch to fetch basic metadata. A NAC Wave 2 issues
+		 * twelve GET_REPORTs walking report IDs 1..12 (lengths 6, 10,
+		 * 14, 18, 26, 50, 96, 194, 258, 386, 514, 768) and an MBUX
+		 * issues SET_REPORT with report ID 0x0f; both get stalled, and
+		 * both head units then stop making progress. See catplay issue
+		 * #11 ("MBUX doesn't role switch because of missing iAP2-over-
+		 * HID support") and catplay-firmware issue #1.
+		 *
+		 * Answering these properly means running an iAP2 pipe before the
+		 * role switch in addition to the one after it, which is well
+		 * beyond what this patch should attempt. Answering them wrongly
+		 * - an empty report, the way Android's f_hid does - would hand
+		 * the head unit a malformed iAP2 packet instead of an honest
+		 * "not supported", which may be worse. So the behaviour is left
+		 * alone deliberately and only made visible.
+		 */
+		pr_info("iPhone: unhandled class request bmReq=0x%02x bReq=0x%02x - stalling",
+				ctrl->bRequestType, ctrl->bRequest);
+		return -EOPNOTSUPP;
+	}
+
+	pr_info("iPhone: unhandled request bmReq=0x%02x bReq=0x%02x - stalling",
+			ctrl->bRequestType, ctrl->bRequest);
 	return -EOPNOTSUPP;
 }
 
--
2.55.0
